Backport of upstream strtod fixes for kParseFullPrecisionFlag (CONTRIB-4780).

Upstream:
  https://github.com/Tencent/rapidjson/pull/1290 (Fix strtod, merge 6cc3910a10bf)
  https://github.com/Tencent/rapidjson/pull/2326 (Fix parsing 0.184467440737095516159, d621dc9e9c77)

Taken from #1290: internal/strtod.h, internal/diyfp.h, internal/biginteger.h.
Not taken: the reader.h part of #1290 (it also changes parsing without kParseFullPrecisionFlag),
templating over Ch (upstream b952a592a4), clzll, MSVC and other compiler guard changes.

Deviations from upstream:
  * RAPIDJSON_ASSERT(exp >= INT_MIN + dExpAdjust) in StrtodFullPrecision is replaced
    by an early return of 0.0, because upstream 8269bc2bc289 (negative exponent clamping
    in reader.h) that guarantees it is not backported.
  * When StrtodDiyFp overflows to infinity and is not sure about rounding, the
    approximation is clamped to DBL_MAX before StrtodBigInteger. Upstream master
    returns NaN for such inputs, e.g. 9.0154242660317648163e308.
  * Digits beyond 768 are replaced by a sticky digit 1 in StrtodBigInteger, so an
    exact halfway followed by a non-zero tail rounds up. Upstream master rounds such
    inputs to even (the "round up" TODO in upstream readertest.cpp).
  * Double::IsNormal() is false for zero, so CheckWithinHalfULP treats an approximation
    of 0.0 as a denormal. Upstream master returns 4.9e-324 for values at or just below
    2^-1075, e.g. 2.47032822920623272088e-324 (the other TODO in upstream readertest.cpp).
